WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.159
Episode fifteen: write a plugin, a history-matching algorithm.

2
00:00:04.159 --> 00:00:14.507
This video assumes APEX installed, as in episode one, and shows the plugin steps of episode fourteen more briefly.

3
00:00:14.507 --> 00:00:19.661
A history-matching method is a small state machine that APEX's workflow drives.

4
00:00:19.661 --> 00:00:22.336
The method never runs a simulator itself.

5
00:00:22.336 --> 00:00:26.120
The example is ES-MDA with a geometric inflation schedule.

6
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:41.707
It goes in a package of its own.

7
00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:58.932
Its inflation factors decrease geometrically, so the early updates are damped and the last ones refine.

8
00:00:58.932 --> 00:01:03.464
Start asks for the prior's forecast, and advance takes the forecasts back.

9
00:01:03.464 --> 00:01:33.560
It asks for forecasts, and the workflow runs them on the configured simulator and executor, with failed-member handling, checkpoints, metrics, progress and cross validation.

10
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.208
Two rules keep runs exact.

11
00:01:35.208 --> 00:01:39.392
Draw random numbers only from setup.streams, never from np.random.

12
00:01:39.392 --> 00:01:46.683
Put everything the loop needs into export_state(), including a pending update whose forecast has not come back yet.

13
00:01:46.683 --> 00:02:04.363
Restore state then continues from a checkpoint.

14
00:02:04.363 --> 00:02:23.921
The update itself comes from APEX: the method calls an update strategy, here approx, and keeps only its own loop.

15
00:02:23.921 --> 00:02:36.200
We register it in pyproject.toml, as for the metric, with the entry point in apex.methods.

16
00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:49.497
From the APEX folder, we install the package into APEX's environment.

17
00:02:49.497 --> 00:02:52.034
We check that APEX finds the method.

18
00:02:52.034 --> 00:03:03.627
Its status, experimental, is shown in apex plugins.

19
00:03:03.627 --> 00:03:12.835
We go to the linear-Gaussian example's folder.

20
00:03:12.835 --> 00:03:20.681
The page says: copy the original config_esmda.toml (not the metric's copy) to config_geo.toml, give its run a name of its own.

21
00:03:20.681 --> 00:03:25.414
Then replace its [method] section with this one, which names the new method.

22
00:03:25.414 --> 00:03:36.860
We did the copy, and removed its old [method] section, before this scene.

23
00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:37.974
We validate it.

24
00:03:37.974 --> 00:03:55.060
The plan should say at most 5 forecasts, 2,500 simulator runs: the method's own count.

25
00:03:55.060 --> 00:04:08.961
Then we run it, like any of APEX's own methods.

26
00:04:08.961 --> 00:04:28.468
The report shows the schedule (in the damping column) and the data mismatch falling.

27
00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:30.086
Does it resume exactly?

28
00:04:30.086 --> 00:04:35.079
Stop a run early, resume it, and compare it with the uninterrupted run.

29
00:04:35.079 --> 00:04:48.622
First, two steps of a copy.

30
00:04:48.622 --> 00:04:49.725
Then we resume it.

31
00:04:49.725 --> 00:04:56.401
Every checkpoint must be identical to the uninterrupted run's; the page has a short check that compares them.

32
00:04:56.401 --> 00:05:09.884
A method needs an independent reference.

33
00:05:09.884 --> 00:05:15.384
Thank you for watching.
